DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  ttaabbss program clears and sets tab-stops on the terminal.  This uses
       the terminfo cclleeaarr__aallll__ttaabbss and sseett__ttaabb capabilities.  If either is ab‐
       sent,  ttaabbss  is  unable to clear/set tab-stops.  The terminal should be
       configured to use hard tabs, e.g.,

           stty tab0

       Like cclleeaarr(1), ttaabbss writes to the standard output.   You  can  redirect
       the standard output to a file (which prevents ttaabbss from actually chang‐
       ing the tabstops), and later ccaatt the file to the screen,  setting  tab‐
       stops at that point.

       These  are  hardware  tabs, which cannot be queried rapidly by applica‐
       tions running in the terminal, if at all.  Curses and other full-screen
       applications  may  use  hardware tabs in optimizing their output to the
       terminal.  If the hardware tabstops differ from the information in  the
       terminal  database, the result is unpredictable.  Before running curses
       programs, you should either reset tab-stops to the standard interval

           tabs -8

       or use the rreesseett program, since the normal initialization sequences  do
       not ensure that tab-stops are reset.

