       The  --dd  (debug)  and --nn (no-op) options are extensions not provided by
       other implementations.

       A ttaabbss utility appeared in PWB/Unix 1.0 (1977).  There  was  a  reduced
       version  of  the  ttaabbss  utility in Unix 7th edition and in 3BSD (1979).
       The latter supported a single “-n” option (to cause the first tab  stop
       to be set on the left margin).  That option is not documented by POSIX.

       The  PWB/Unix  ttaabbss  utility,  which was included in System III (1980),
       used built-in tables rather than the terminal database,  to  support  a
       half-dozen  hardcopy  terminal  (printer)  types.  It also had built-in
       logic to support the left-margin, as well as a feature for copying  the
       tab settings from a file.

       Later  versions  of  Unix,  e.g.,  SVr4, added support for the terminal
       database, but kept the tables to support the printers.  In  an  earlier
       development effort, the tab-stop initialization provided by ttsseett (1982)
       and incorporated into ttppuutt uses the terminal database,

       The ++mm option  was  documented  in  the  Base  Specifications  Issue  5
       (Unix98, 1997), and omitted in Issue 6 (Unix03, 2004) without document‐
       ing the rationale, though an introductory comment _“_a_n_d  _o_p_t_i_o_n_a_l_l_y  _a_d_‐
       _j_u_s_t_s  _t_h_e  _m_a_r_g_i_n_” remains, overlooked in the removal.  The documented
       ttaabbss utility in Issues 6 and later has no mechanism  for  setting  mar‐
       gins.  The ++mm option in this implementation differs from the feature in
       SVr4 by using terminal capabilities rather than built-in tables.

       POSIX documents no limits on the number of  tab  stops.   Documentation
       for other implementations states that there is a limit on the number of
       tab stops (e.g., 20 in PWB/Unix's ttaabbss utility).  While some  terminals
       may  not  accept  an arbitrary number of tab stops, this implementation
       will attempt to set tab stops up to the right margin of the screen,  if
       the given list happens to be that long.

       The  _R_a_t_i_o_n_a_l_e section of the POSIX documentation goes into some detail
       about the ways the committee considered redesigning the ttaabbss  and  ttppuutt
       utilities, without proposing an improved solution.  It comments that

            no  known  historical  version  of tabs supports the capability of
            setting arbitrary tab stops.

       However, the _E_x_p_l_i_c_i_t _L_i_s_t_s described in this manual page  were  imple‐
       mented  in  PWB/Unix.  Those provide the capability of setting abitrary
       tab stops.

